  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Born in Burnham-on-Crouch and raised partly in Venezuela, de Savary left school with few qualifications and worked in Canada before returning to Britain. He borrowed money to buy a Dutch import-export concern in 1969, expanded it through African trade and later moved into oil, banking and private clubs. St James’s Club established the hospitality formula that became a recurring centre of his career.\n\nDe Savary repeatedly acquired distinctive properties and remade them as hotels, clubs or destinations, including Skibo Castle, Bovey Castle, Land’s End and John o’Groats. A severe corporate collapse forced him to sell major assets, but he rebuilt through luxury hotels, golf courses and marinas across Britain, the Caribbean and North America. His parallel sailing career produced Britain’s 1983 America’s Cup campaign and Pendennis Shipyard, which grew into an enduring Cornish shipbuilder.",
